T1  - A Structural Equation Model Analysis on Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder, Childhood Trauma, and Substance Misuse Among Female Adolescents in the Juvenile Justice System
T3  - The 2024 Annual Meeting of the College on Problems of Drug Dependence. Montreal Canada.
A1  - Wiedbusch E.
A1  - Suarez J.-A.
A1  - Johnson M.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Drug Category: Other, Adolescent Substance Misuse Treatment Topic: Criminal Justice Abstract Detail: Other Abstract Category: Original Research Aim: Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), childhood trauma, and substance misuse are highly correlated and overrepresented among female adolescents within the juvenile justice system. Additionally, trauma can emulate ADHD symptoms, posing challenges for behavioral health treatment. Identifying whether specific conditions, if any, pose a greater risk or contribute as compounding factors to substance misuse is critical for prevention and early intervention programs. With a focus on an understudied population-female adolescents in the juvenile justice system-this study aims to inform targeted prevention and early intervention strategies for their unique challenges. <ovid:br/>Method(s): Structural equation modeling was employed to analyze 8,253 female adolescents drawn from a statewide sample of 36,754 adolescents involved in the Florida Department of Juvenile Justice. The model, guided by extant literature, examined the direct and moderated effects of ADHD and childhood trauma on substance misuse. The measurement model for childhood trauma was fitted to the data before being added to the structural model. Substance misuse was measured through participation in a drug treatment program; the dichotomous ADHD variable distinguished between female adolescents with and without a history of diagnosis and treatment. Control variables align with prior studies. <ovid:br/>Result(s): Preliminary results indicate that both history of ADHD and childhood trauma had significant direct effects on substance misuse (p >0.05). Additionally, trauma significantly moderated the effect of ADHD on substance misuse (p >0.05). However, the direct and moderated impact of childhood trauma on substance misuse varied by the severity of the trauma. Control variables will contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of these dynamics. <ovid:br/>Conclusion(s): Preliminary findings underscore the significant impact of ADHD and childhood trauma on substance misuse among female adolescents in the juvenile justice system. Ongoing analysis promises further insights into the nuanced interplay between ADHD, trauma, and substance misuse, informing targeted prevention and intervention strategies for this understudied population. Financial Support: The research reported in this publication was supported by the National Institutes of Health under award numbers 1K01DA052679 (Dr. Micah E. Johnson, PI), R25DA050735 (Dr. Micah E. Johnson, PI, and Kimberly Johnson, co-PI), and R25DA059073 (Dr. Micah E. Johnson, MPI and contact PI). The content is solely the authors' responsibility and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health or the Florida Department of Juvenile Justice.<ovid:br/>Copyright &#xa9; 2024
