A1  - Amanullah S.
A1  - Varma K.
A1  - Singh J.
A1  - Upadhyay V.K.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: ADHD is underdiagnosed in females, with childhood diagnosis rates showing a male-to-female ratio of 4:1, narrowing to 1:1 in adulthood. This disparity stems from factors like symptom masking, inattentive presentations, delayed onset, and diagnostic criteria based on predominantly male samples. Females often exhibit internal symptoms such as daydreaming or mind-wandering, which are less overt than hyperactive behaviors. <ovid:br/>Aim(s): To examine factors contributing to ADHD underdiagnosis in females. <ovid:br/>Method(s): A literature review (2014-2024) used databases like PubMed and Google Scholar with keywords related to ADHD in females. Filters included open access, full text, and English language. <ovid:br/>Result(s): Findings highlight underdiagnosis due to gender roles, genetic influences, symptom masking, and male-biased diagnostic criteria. <ovid:br/>Conclusion(s): Addressing ADHD diagnostic disparities in females requires gender-sensitive criteria and further research to enhance timely recognition.
