T1  - PMH27 ADHD TREATMENT AND DIAGNOSIS AMONG TEENAGE AND COLLEGE-AGED WOMEN
T3  - ISPOR 2020. Orlando United States.
A1  - Bloemers S.
A1  - Mummert A.
A1  - Gibson T.B.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Objectives: Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) diagnosis among females has the potential for under diagnosis. When untreated, ADHD can result in significant impairments to academic achievement. Female college students may be particularly vulnerable to these impairments due to prior missed diagnosis. Thus, it is important to document ADHD rates in this otherwise understudied population. <ovid:br/>Method(s): We examined diagnosis and treatment rates among females aged 12-17 (teenaged) or 19-25 (college-aged) using the IBM MarketScan Commercial Database. Women aged 18 years were excluded and those with prior ADHD diagnosis or treatment. Females were included if newly prescribed an ADHD medication during 2018 (treatment) or in 2018 had a new ADHD diagnosis (ICD-10-CM codes F90.0, F90.1, F90.2, F90.8, F90.9). Inclusion required continuous enrollment of one year before and after index diagnosis or treatment date. Differences in new ADHD rates between age groups were examined using two proportion z test. P values < 0.05 were considered statistically significant. <ovid:br/>Result(s): For ADHD treatment or diagnosis we found 3,179 females age 12-17 that met the study criteria. Among those 66% had a diagnosis of ADHD and 69% were prescribed an ADHD medication. Ages 19-25 included 3,444 females and 60% had an ADHD diagnosis and 84% were prescribed an ADHD medication. When comparing overall onset rates for age groups 12-17 and 19-25, the older group exhibited higher rates of new ADHD diagnosis and treatment (p<0.01). When comparing ages 17 to 19, there was also a significant difference in diagnosis and treatment rates with the 19-year-old age group demonstrating higher rates (p<0.01). <ovid:br/>Conclusion(s): This study demonstrated higher rates of new diagnosis and treatment for ADHD among college age women. <ovid:br/>Result(s): from this study can influence practice patterns related to detecting ADHD in females, as earlier detection has the potential to offset consequences stemming from ADHD.<ovid:br/>Copyright &#xa9; 2020
