T1  - Astrocytes regulate inhibitory system distinctively on male and female in ADHD mouse model
T3  - IBRO World Congress 2019 - Symposia. Daegu South Korea.
A1  - Kim G.Y.
A1  - Yoon B.-E.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is diagnosed approximately three times more often in boys than in girls, what makes differences and which is different in gender are unknown. In this study, we used ADHD mouse model (GIT1<ovid:sup>+/-</ovid:sup> mouse) separated developmentally (neonatal (P0-P2) and juvenile (4-5w)) and brain-regionally (cortex, hippocampus and cerebellum) to identify the differences in gender. We analyzed pro-inflammatory cytokine (IL-1beta, IL-6, TNF-alpha) expression in tissue for investigating the effect on brain development and neurotransmitter regulation. We found cytokines have own expression patterns depend on developmental stage and brain regions. Most of cytokines increased in juvenile ADHD mice, especially elevated IL-6 and TNF-alpha can regulate inhibitory neurotransmitter release. Therefore, we investigated GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) and MAOB (monoamine oxidase B) as factors that could affect inhibitory system. In ADHD mice, astrocytes according to regions have distinct GABA expression depends on gender and maintained from neonatal to juvenile ADHD in cerebellum. Additionally, astrocytic GABA is consistent with IL-6 expression in cerebellum. To examine behavioral differences, we performed open-field test and passive avoidance test. ADHD female significantly showed short-term memory impairment. Altogether, we suggest that astrocytic IL-6 can make gender differences in regulation of inhibitory system in cerebellum of ADHD.<ovid:br/>Copyright &#xa9; 2019
